#!/usr/bin/python3
"""gnome-terminal font size by monitor.

What it does
    A gnome-terminal window gets the font size of the monitor it sits on:
        the laptop's own panel (eDP, 1920x1200)       11pt   "Laptop screen" profile
        lower resolution: longer side under 2560 px
            (the 1920x1080 HDMI monitor)               9pt   "Small screen" profile
        anything else (the 2560x1440 monitors)        10pt   the default profile
    A window switches about half a second after it stops moving; a new window or tab
    about a tenth of a second after it appears (after the last one, when several appear
    in quick succession).

How it works
    A terminal draws all of its text in one font size, and gnome-terminal sets the font
    per profile. (Its per-tab zoom goes in 1.2x steps, 10pt to 8.3pt or 12pt, so it can't
    give 9pt or 11pt.) So this script keeps a profile for each extra size (SIZED_PROFILES)
    and switches each window between those and the default profile through
    gnome-terminal's D-Bus interface. That is the same action as the window menu's
    Terminal > Change Profile.

    - The sized profiles mirror the default profile: every setting except the name, the
      font and use-system-font is copied from it, at start and whenever the default
      profile, a sized profile, the profile list or the system monospace font changes.
      Each one's font is the default profile's font (its own, or the system monospace
      font if it uses that) at the sized profile's own size. The sizes are absolute, not
      relative to the default font: with a 9pt default font, "Small screen" would change
      nothing. Edit the default profile in Preferences; changes made to a sized profile
      are undone straight away, and a deleted one comes back.
    - The monitor that counts is the one under the window's top-left corner, not its
      centre: the corner of its contents, just below the title bar, or of gnome-terminal's
      own header bar if it uses one. Switching the font resizes a window that isn't
      maximized: gnome-terminal keeps its rows and columns, so the window grows or shrinks
      to the right and down while the top-left corner stays put. Measured from the centre,
      that resize could push the window over a monitor edge, which would switch it back,
      and so on. (A maximized window, restored after moving to another monitor, gets back
      its old size in pixels, not its old rows and columns.)
    - When a grown window no longer fits, the window manager pushes it back onto the
      monitor or shrinks it, and the rows it loses don't come back. That is likeliest on
      the laptop panel, which fits the fewest rows. A window dropped right at a monitor
      edge can be pushed across it; it then switches once more and stays.
    - Windows are only switched between the sized profiles and the default profile (or
      one that was the default earlier in this run). A window put on any other profile by
      hand (Terminal > Change Profile) is left alone; one put on a sized or the default
      profile by hand goes back to the one its monitor calls for.
    - Only the active tab switches; a background tab switches when it becomes active.
    - It sleeps until something happens: a window opened or moved, a tab opened or
      switched, a monitor changed, a profile or the system monospace font edited. A
      moving window is left alone until it has stood still for SETTLE_MS, so it doesn't
      switch back and forth mid-drag; dragging one window doesn't hold up another. A
      monitor change is also checked after SETTLE_MS, anything else (such as a new window
      or tab) after SOON_MS.

Installing
    It belongs with the rest of the shell setup in the dotfiles repo (~/Documents/dotfiles,
    github.com/solingenn/dotfiles). Like the other dotfiles it is copied into place, not
    symlinked:
        ~/.local/bin/terminal-font-by-monitor    this file, executable (chmod +x)
        ~/.config/autostart/terminal-font-by-monitor.desktop    starts it at login:
            [Desktop Entry]
            Type=Application
            Name=Terminal font by monitor
            Exec=/home/mario_nardi/.local/bin/terminal-font-by-monitor
            X-GNOME-Autostart-enabled=true
    Exec needs the absolute path (desktop files don't expand ~ or $HOME), so fix the
    home directory in it on a machine with another user name. The script creates the
    sized profiles itself; there is nothing to set up in gnome-terminal.

Requirements
    An X11 session (Cinnamon here). It doesn't work on Wayland, where a program can't see
    other windows' positions. Whole-number display scaling only: with fractional scaling
    a monitor reports a scaled size and can get the wrong font. Debian/Ubuntu packages:
    python3-gi, gir1.2-gtk-3.0, gir1.2-wnck-3.0, gir1.2-pango-1.0, python3-xlib,
    gnome-terminal. Written on Linux Mint 22.3 (Python 3.12, GNOME Terminal 3.52,
    libwnck 43, PyGObject 3.48).

Running and debugging
    pgrep -aLF "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/terminal-font-by-monitor.lock"    is it running?
        (When it isn't, pgrep says "pidfile not valid".)
    pkill -LF "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/terminal-font-by-monitor.lock"    stop it
    terminal-font-by-monitor -v    run it in a terminal, logging every check
    Only one copy runs at a time and a second one exits with an error, so stop the
    autostarted copy before running it with -v. Stopping it leaves every window on its
    current profile.

    The lock file holds the running copy's process id. -L makes pgrep and pkill check
    that the file is still locked, so a file left over from an earlier run can't point
    them at some other process. (Avoid `pkill -f terminal-font-by-monitor`: it also
    kills anything else with that name on its command line, such as an editor or the
    shell running the command.)

    Started by autostart, it logs to ~/.xsession-errors, each line starting with the date
    and time to the millisecond:
        grep terminal-font-by-monitor ~/.xsession-errors
    It logs starting, stopping, every switch and every change it makes to the profiles,
    and the warnings of the libraries it uses (GTK, dconf, ...). A window closing or
    gnome-terminal quitting mid-check is normal and logged only with -v. Any other error
    talking to gnome-terminal is logged as a warning, once per kind, since it may mean an
    update changed what this script relies on (see "behaviour this relies on" in the
    code). While such an error lasts, the windows are checked again every ERROR_RETRY_MS.
"""

import argparse
import ctypes
import fcntl
import logging
import os
import signal
import sys
import time
from collections.abc import Callable, Sequence
from dataclasses import dataclass
from functools import partial
from pathlib import Path
from types import TracebackType
from typing import ClassVar, NoReturn, TextIO, override

# --- Logging ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set up before the imports below, so that a missing package is logged like the rest.

APP_NAME = "terminal-font-by-monitor"
log = logging.getLogger(APP_NAME)


class EveryLineFormatter(logging.Formatter):
    """Starts every line of an entry, traceback lines too, with time, program and level.

    So `grep terminal-font-by-monitor ~/.xsession-errors` finds all of it, and every line
    says when it happened.
    """

    @override
    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:
        time_text = f"{self.formatTime(record, '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')}.{int(record.msecs):03d}"
        prefix = f"{time_text} {record.name}: {record.levelname}: "
        return "\n".join(prefix + line for line in super().format(record).splitlines())


def log_uncaught(exc_type: type[BaseException], exc_value: BaseException,
                 tb: TracebackType | None) -> None:
    """sys.excepthook: an error that escapes a callback goes to the log, not bare stderr."""
    log.critical("unexpected error", exc_info=(exc_type, exc_value, tb))


def setup_logging() -> None:
    """Log to stderr (~/.xsession-errors when autostart starts it), one prefix per line.

    At INFO level; main lowers it to DEBUG for -v once it has read the options.
    """
    handler = logging.StreamHandler()
    handler.setFormatter(EveryLineFormatter("%(message)s"))
    logging.basicConfig(handlers=[handler], level=logging.INFO)
    # PyGObject reports an exception raised in a callback through sys.excepthook.
    sys.excepthook = log_uncaught


try:
    import gi
    from Xlib import X
    from Xlib.display import Display as XDisplay
    from Xlib.error import BadWindow, DisplayError, XError

    # gi needs the library versions pinned before importing them.
    gi.require_version("Gdk", "3.0")
    gi.require_version("GdkX11", "3.0")
    gi.require_version("Gtk", "3.0")
    gi.require_version("Pango", "1.0")
    gi.require_version("Wnck", "3.0")
    from gi.repository import Gdk, GdkX11, Gio, GLib, Gtk, Pango, Wnck
except (ImportError, ValueError) as error:  # ValueError: gi has no such library
    # A package is missing (see Requirements), the likeliest failure on a new machine.
    setup_logging()
    log.error("%s (a package is missing; see Requirements at the top of this file)", error)
    sys.exit(1)

# --- Settings you may want to change ----------------------------------------------------


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class SizedProfile:
    """A copy of the default profile with its own font size, for some monitors."""

    name: str
    # Fixed, so every run (and every machine these dotfiles go to) reuses the same
    # profile rather than adding a new one.
    uuid: str
    # In points; the font itself is the default profile's. Use whole sizes: 9.5 rendered
    # squashed.
    font_size: int


# The laptop's own panel: small but sharp, so a bigger font than its resolution suggests.
LAPTOP_PROFILE = SizedProfile("Laptop screen", "4d3192c9-80ba-40a8-88e0-dcfeb03ca01a", 11)
# Lower-resolution monitors.
SMALL_PROFILE = SizedProfile("Small screen", "61d9462b-c446-41e7-a061-25edb620e115", 9)
# Every profile profile_for returns must be in here, or it isn't mirrored.
SIZED_PROFILES = (LAPTOP_PROFILE, SMALL_PROFILE)

# Connector-name prefixes that mark a laptop's own panel. xrandr shows it as eDP, eDP-1,
# LVDS1, DSI-1, ... depending on the driver; some, NVIDIA's for one, use a plain DP-<n>:
# add that name here, bearing in mind that "DP-1" also matches "DP-10".
LAPTOP_PANEL_CONNECTORS = ("eDP", "LVDS", "DSI")
# Other monitors whose longer side has fewer pixels than this count as lower resolution.
# (The longer side, so a monitor rotated to portrait counts the same.)
SMALL_BELOW_PX = 2560
# Dragging a window sends a stream of move events; act once they have paused this long.
# Also the wait after a monitor change, and before a second try at an ignored switch.
SETTLE_MS = 500
# After an error talking to gnome-terminal other than a window closing (a timeout, say),
# check the windows again this often until it works.
ERROR_RETRY_MS = 10_000
# New windows and tabs open on whichever profile they were given; correct them this
# soon. Not zero, so a burst (several tabs opening at once) is handled in one pass.
SOON_MS = 100

# --- Program ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# How long to wait for gnome-terminal to answer a D-Bus call.
DBUS_TIMEOUT_MS = 2000
# A new window's first position change this soon after it appears is the window manager
# placing it (Muffin: 4-19 ms after Wnck reports the window); a later one is a move.
PLACEMENT_MS = 50
# GLib's message levels, most severe first, and the levels they go to the log at. (GLib's
# "error" is fatal; its "critical" means a bug.) Its info and debug messages are left to
# GLib, which shows them only with G_MESSAGES_DEBUG set.
GLIB_LOG_LEVELS = (
    (GLib.LogLevelFlags.LEVEL_ERROR, logging.CRITICAL),
    (GLib.LogLevelFlags.LEVEL_CRITICAL, logging.ERROR),
    (GLib.LogLevelFlags.LEVEL_WARNING, logging.WARNING),
    (GLib.LogLevelFlags.LEVEL_MESSAGE, logging.INFO),
)

# --- gnome-terminal, GTK and X11 behaviour this relies on -------------------------------
# If an update breaks one of these, missing settings stop the script with an error, and
# a changed D-Bus action or reply logs a warning (once per kind). The rest fail quietly,
# logged with -v at most: windows just don't switch, or switch wrongly.

# Holds the uuids of every profile and of the default profile.
PROFILES_LIST_SCHEMA = "org.gnome.Terminal.ProfilesList"
LIST_KEY = "list"
DEFAULT_KEY = "default"
# The settings of one profile. The schema is relocatable: each profile keeps its own copy
# under a dconf path named after its uuid.
PROFILE_SCHEMA = "org.gnome.Terminal.Legacy.Profile"
PROFILE_PATH = "/org/gnome/terminal/legacy/profiles:/:{uuid}/"
VISIBLE_NAME_KEY = "visible-name"
USE_SYSTEM_FONT_KEY = "use-system-font"
FONT_KEY = "font"
# Keys a sized profile sets itself instead of copying them from the default profile
# (ProfileMirror._own_values).
OWN_KEYS = (VISIBLE_NAME_KEY, USE_SYSTEM_FONT_KEY, FONT_KEY)
# The system monospace font, used by profiles with use-system-font.
INTERFACE_SCHEMA = "org.gnome.desktop.interface"
MONOSPACE_FONT_KEY = "monospace-font-name"
# Family for a font description that names none (VTE falls back to it as well).
FALLBACK_FAMILY = "Monospace"
# WM_CLASS instance name of terminal windows. (Preferences is a separate process,
# gnome-terminal-preferences, with its own name.)
TERMINAL_WM_INSTANCE = "gnome-terminal-server"
# GTK publishes each application window's actions (what its menus do) on D-Bus under
# this interface, at <application path>/window/<n>, e.g. /org/gnome/Terminal/window/1.
# "profile" is a stateful action whose state is the active tab's profile uuid (type s).
# Setting the state switches the tab to that profile before the D-Bus call returns, or
# does nothing if gnome-terminal doesn't know the uuid. TerminalWindow.active_profile
# checks the reply's type.
ACTIONS_INTERFACE = "org.gtk.Actions"
PROFILE_ACTION = "profile"
# Changed(as removed, a{sb} enabled_changes, a{sv} state_changes, a{s(bgav)} added)
ACTIONS_CHANGED_SIGNATURE = "(asa{sb}a{sv}a{s(bgav)})"
# X11 properties GTK puts on each application window: where its actions live on D-Bus.
GTK_BUS_NAME_PROPERTY = "_GTK_UNIQUE_BUS_NAME"
GTK_WINDOW_PATH_PROPERTY = "_GTK_WINDOW_OBJECT_PATH"
# With client-side decorations (gnome-terminal's header bar), GTK draws an invisible shadow
# margin around the window and gives its size in this property: left, right, top, bottom.
GTK_FRAME_EXTENTS_PROPERTY = "_GTK_FRAME_EXTENTS"
X_STRING_FORMAT = 8  # X11 property format of strings: 8 bits per item
X_CARDINAL_FORMAT = 32  # ... and of numbers
# X11 request numbers from here up are extensions'; the minor number then says which of the
# extension's requests it was.
X_FIRST_EXTENSION_REQUEST = 128
# D-Bus errors that only mean a window or gnome-terminal went away between an event and the
# check: a closed window answers UNKNOWN_METHOD, a quit gnome-terminal SERVICE_UNKNOWN.
GONE_DBUS_ERRORS = (
    Gio.DBusError.SERVICE_UNKNOWN, Gio.DBusError.NAME_HAS_NO_OWNER,
    Gio.DBusError.UNKNOWN_METHOD, Gio.DBusError.UNKNOWN_OBJECT,
    Gio.DBusError.UNKNOWN_INTERFACE,
)
# On X11, GDK names each monitor ("model") after its RandR monitor, which for an ordinary
# monitor is its output's connector name (eDP, HDMI-A-0, ...): that is how the laptop
# panel is recognised (X11Windows.monitor).

# The log domain of dconf's messages, such as "failed to commit changes to dconf".
DCONF_LOG_DOMAIN = "dconf"


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Monitor:
    """A monitor, as far as choosing a profile goes."""

    connector: str  # the name of its output: eDP, HDMI-A-0, ...
    width_px: int
    height_px: int

    @override
    def __str__(self) -> str:
        return f"{self.connector} ({self.width_px}x{self.height_px})"


def profile_for(monitor: Monitor) -> SizedProfile | None:
    """The sized profile for a monitor, or None for the default profile."""
    if monitor.connector.startswith(LAPTOP_PANEL_CONNECTORS):
        return LAPTOP_PROFILE
    if max(monitor.width_px, monitor.height_px) < SMALL_BELOW_PX:
        return SMALL_PROFILE
    return None


def with_size(font: str, points: int) -> str:
    """The Pango font description `font` resized to `points` points.

    A description without a family gets FALLBACK_FAMILY.
    """
    description = Pango.FontDescription.from_string(font)
    if not description.get_family():
        description.set_family(FALLBACK_FAMILY)
    description.set_size(points * Pango.SCALE)
    return description.to_string()


def profile_settings(uuid: str) -> Gio.Settings:
    """The dconf-backed settings of the gnome-terminal profile with this uuid."""
    return Gio.Settings.new_with_path(PROFILE_SCHEMA, PROFILE_PATH.format(uuid=uuid))


def settings_problem() -> str | None:
    """Why the settings this script needs can't be used here, or None if they can.

    GLib treats a missing settings schema or key as a programming error and aborts the
    process, so check before touching them.
    """
    source = Gio.SettingsSchemaSource.get_default()
    if source is None:
        return "no settings schemas are installed"
    needed = {
        PROFILES_LIST_SCHEMA: (LIST_KEY, DEFAULT_KEY),
        PROFILE_SCHEMA: OWN_KEYS,
        INTERFACE_SCHEMA: (MONOSPACE_FONT_KEY,),
    }
    for schema_id, keys in needed.items():
        schema = source.lookup(schema_id, recursive=True)
        if schema is None:
            return f"settings schema {schema_id} is not installed (is gnome-terminal?)"
        missing = [key for key in keys if not schema.has_key(key)]
        if missing:
            return f"settings schema {schema_id} has no {', '.join(missing)}"
    return None


class UnexpectedReply(Exception):
    """gnome-terminal sent a reply this script can't read (did an update change it?)."""


class WindowGone(Exception):
    """The window closed while it was being looked up."""


def window_gone(error: Exception) -> bool:
    """Whether an error only means that the window, or gnome-terminal, has gone away."""
    if isinstance(error, BadWindow | WindowGone):
        return True
    return isinstance(error, GLib.Error) and any(
        error.matches(Gio.DBusError.quark(), code) for code in GONE_DBUS_ERRORS)


def describe(error: Exception) -> str:
    """An error as the log shows it; also how _warn_once tells kinds of error apart.

    python-xlib's own text for an X error is its class followed by every field, including
    the sequence number of the request, so no two would read the same.
    """
    if isinstance(error, XError):
        kind = "of an unknown kind" if type(error) is XError else type(error).__name__
        request = str(error.major_opcode)
        if error.major_opcode >= X_FIRST_EXTENSION_REQUEST:
            request += f".{error.minor_opcode}"
        return f"X error {kind} (code {error.code}, request {request})"
    return str(error)


class Debouncer:
    """Calls a function once a burst of triggers has paused for a while."""

    def __init__(self, callback: Callable[[], None]) -> None:
        self._callback = callback
        self._source = 0  # the pending GLib timeout, or 0

    def trigger(self, delay_ms: int) -> None:
        """Call the function `delay_ms` from now, replacing a pending call."""
        self.cancel()
        self._source = GLib.timeout_add(delay_ms, self._run)

    def cancel(self) -> None:
        """Drop the pending call, if any."""
        if self._source:
            GLib.source_remove(self._source)
            self._source = 0

    @property
    def pending(self) -> bool:
        """Whether a call is due."""
        return self._source != 0

    def _run(self) -> bool:
        self._source = 0
        self._callback()
        return GLib.SOURCE_REMOVE  # one-shot


class ProfileMirror:
    """Keeps each sized profile a copy of the default profile, apart from name and font.

    start does a first sync, and from then on any change to the profile list, the default
    profile, a sized profile or the system monospace font schedules a full sync (_sync).
    A sync puts missing sized profiles back in the list, follows a change of default
    profile, sets each sized profile's own keys (_own_values) and copies every other key
    from the default profile. It writes only values that differ, so the changes it causes
    itself lead to one more sync that finds nothing to do.
    """

    # Wait this long for a burst of changes to end: Preferences writes keys one at a time.
    SYNC_DELAY_MS: ClassVar[int] = 200
    # When the mirror's writes keep being undone (dconf failing to save, or another program
    # writing the same keys), the delay doubles with every sync that has to redo a write
    # (backoff), up to this: seldom enough not to keep dconf and gnome-terminal busy, often
    # enough that the profiles are right again soon after the cause goes away. Backoff only
    # delays syncs for changes to what the mirror writes (the profile list, the sized
    # profiles). Changes to what it only reads (which profile is the default, that profile,
    # the system monospace font) are never its own echo and always sync after
    # SYNC_DELAY_MS.
    MAX_SYNC_DELAY_MS: ClassVar[int] = 30_000
    # Log a warning once this many syncs in a streak had to redo a write.
    WARN_AFTER_UNDONE_SYNCS: ClassVar[int] = 3
    # A streak of syncs redoing writes ends once the mirror has written nothing for this
    # long. Longer than MAX_SYNC_DELAY_MS, so that syncs at that delay stay one streak.
    CALM_MS: ClassVar[int] = 2 * MAX_SYNC_DELAY_MS

    def __init__(self, sized_profiles: Sequence[SizedProfile]) -> None:
        self.default_uuid = ""
        # Profiles windows may be switched between: the sized profiles and every profile
        # that was the default during this run (windows still on an earlier default would
        # otherwise count as picked by hand).
        self.managed_uuids = {profile.uuid for profile in sized_profiles}
        # See start.
        self._on_profiles_changed: Callable[[], None] = lambda: None

        self._profiles = Gio.Settings.new(PROFILES_LIST_SCHEMA)
        self._copies = [(profile, profile_settings(profile.uuid))
                        for profile in sized_profiles]
        self._interface = Gio.Settings.new(INTERFACE_SCHEMA)
        self._default: Gio.Settings | None = None
        self._default_handler = 0
        self._rejected_default = ""  # last unusable default, so it is logged only once
        self._debouncer = Debouncer(self._sync)
        # Whether the pending sync is for an edit (_on_source_changed), and so due soon.
        self._edit_pending = False
        # What the current sync and the last sync that wrote anything wrote, as (dconf path,
        # key, value).
        self._writes: set[tuple[str, str, str | None]] = set()
        self._previous_writes: set[tuple[str, str, str | None]] = set()
        # Syncs in the current streak that redid a write, and the backoff: how long a
        # change to what the mirror writes waits for its sync (see _track_undone_writes).
        self._undone_syncs = 0
        self._backoff_ms = self.SYNC_DELAY_MS
        self._calm = Debouncer(self._calm_down)

    def start(self, on_profiles_changed: Callable[[], None]) -> None:
        """Sync now, then whenever something changes.

        `on_profiles_changed` is called when windows need another check: the default
        profile changed, or a sized profile was put back in the list (gnome-terminal
        ignores switches to a profile that isn't in it).
        """
        self._on_profiles_changed = on_profiles_changed
        # GSettings only reports changes to keys that were read while a "changed" handler
        # was connected, so connect first: the sync below reads every key.
        self._profiles.connect(f"changed::{LIST_KEY}", self._on_written_changed)
        self._profiles.connect(f"changed::{DEFAULT_KEY}", self._on_source_changed)
        for _, settings in self._copies:
            settings.connect("changed", self._on_written_changed)
        self._interface.connect(f"changed::{MONOSPACE_FONT_KEY}", self._on_source_changed)
        self._sync()

    @property
    def fighting(self) -> bool:
        """Whether writes have kept being undone for long enough to have warned about it."""
        return self._undone_syncs >= self.WARN_AFTER_UNDONE_SYNCS

    def quiet_level(self, level: int) -> int:
        """`level`, or DEBUG while the mirror is fighting.

        For messages that every redone write would log again, without end.
        """
        return logging.DEBUG if self.fighting else level

    def _on_written_changed(self, _settings: Gio.Settings, _key: str) -> None:
        """A change to settings the mirror writes: maybe its own echo, maybe undone."""
        if self._edit_pending:
            # The sync for that edit, due soon, covers this change too; the backoff mustn't
            # put it off (`dconf load` can change the default profile and the list
            # together).
            return
        self._debouncer.trigger(self._backoff_ms)

    def _on_source_changed(self, _settings: Gio.Settings, _key: str) -> None:
        """A change to settings the mirror only reads: someone edited them."""
        self._edit_pending = True
        self._debouncer.trigger(self.SYNC_DELAY_MS)

    def _sync(self) -> None:
        self._edit_pending = False
        self._writes = set()
        uuids, restored = self._restore_list()
        default_changed = self._follow_default(uuids)
        default_font = self._default_font()
        for profile, settings in self._copies:
            self._mirror_into(profile, settings, default_font)
        self._track_undone_writes()
        if restored or default_changed:
            self._on_profiles_changed()

    def _restore_list(self) -> tuple[list[str], bool]:
        """Put missing sized profiles back in the list.

        Returns the list, and whether any profile was put back.
        """
        uuids = self._profiles.get_strv(LIST_KEY)
        missing = [profile for profile, _ in self._copies if profile.uuid not in uuids]
        if missing:
            log.log(self.quiet_level(logging.INFO), "adding profiles: %s",
                    ", ".join(repr(profile.name) for profile in missing))
            uuids = [*uuids, *(profile.uuid for profile in missing)]
            self._set(self._profiles, LIST_KEY, GLib.Variant("as", uuids))
        return uuids, bool(missing)

    def _mirror_into(self, profile: SizedProfile, settings: Gio.Settings,
                     default_font: str) -> None:
        """Make one sized profile a copy of the default profile with its own name and font."""
        own = self._own_values(profile, default_font)
        for key, value in own.items():
            if self._set(settings, key, value) and key == FONT_KEY:
                log.log(self.quiet_level(logging.INFO), "%r font: %s",
                        profile.name, value.get_string())
        if self._default is not None:
            for key in self._default.props.settings_schema.list_keys():
                if key not in own:
                    self._set(settings, key, self._default.get_user_value(key))

    def _track_undone_writes(self) -> None:
        """Count syncs that had to redo a write, for the backoff and the warning.

        The streak, and so the count, ends once the mirror goes CALM_MS without writing.
        """
        if not self._writes:
            # Nothing to write, as in the sync that echoes the mirror's own writes. That
            # they are in place now doesn't mean they will stay (whatever undoes them may
            # be slower), so this sync neither counts nor ends the streak.
            return
        # Writing a key to the same value as the last sync that wrote anything means
        # someone undid that write in between.
        if self._writes & self._previous_writes:
            self._undone_syncs += 1
            self._backoff_ms = min(2 * self._backoff_ms, self.MAX_SYNC_DELAY_MS)
            if self._undone_syncs == self.WARN_AFTER_UNDONE_SYNCS:
                log.warning("profile settings keep reverting (is dconf failing to save?); "
                            "retrying less and less often, down to every %d s, and logging "
                            "the retries with -v only", self.MAX_SYNC_DELAY_MS // 1000)
        self._previous_writes = self._writes
        self._calm.trigger(self.CALM_MS)

    def _calm_down(self) -> None:
        """CALM_MS without writes: the streak is over."""
        if self.fighting:
            log.info("profile settings are saved again")
        self._undone_syncs = 0
        self._backoff_ms = self.SYNC_DELAY_MS
        self._previous_writes = set()

    @staticmethod
    def _own_values(profile: SizedProfile, default_font: str) -> dict[str, GLib.Variant]:
        """The keys a sized profile sets itself, and their values."""
        return {
            VISIBLE_NAME_KEY: GLib.Variant("s", profile.name),
            USE_SYSTEM_FONT_KEY: GLib.Variant("b", False),
            FONT_KEY: GLib.Variant("s", with_size(default_font, profile.font_size)),
        }

    def _default_font(self) -> str:
        """The font the default profile shows: its own, or the system monospace font."""
        if self._default is None or self._default.get_boolean(USE_SYSTEM_FONT_KEY):
            return self._interface.get_string(MONOSPACE_FONT_KEY)
        return self._default.get_string(FONT_KEY)

    def _follow_default(self, uuids: Sequence[str]) -> bool:
        """Start mirroring the default profile if it changed since the last sync.

        `uuids` is the profile list. An unusable default is logged once and ignored, so
        the previous one stays. Returns whether it started mirroring another profile.
        """
        uuid = self._profiles.get_string(DEFAULT_KEY)
        if uuid == self.default_uuid:
            self._rejected_default = ""
            return False
        if (reason := self._unusable_reason(uuid, uuids)) is not None:
            if uuid != self._rejected_default:
                self._rejected_default = uuid
                log.warning("can't mirror default profile %r: %s", uuid, reason)
            return False
        self._rejected_default = ""
        settings = profile_settings(uuid)
        handler = settings.connect("changed", self._on_source_changed)
        if self._default is not None:
            self._default.disconnect(self._default_handler)
        self._default, self._default_handler = settings, handler
        self.default_uuid = uuid
        self.managed_uuids.add(uuid)
        log.info("mirroring default profile %s into %s", uuid,
                 ", ".join(repr(profile.name) for profile, _ in self._copies))
        return True

    def _unusable_reason(self, uuid: str, uuids: Sequence[str]) -> str | None:
        """Why profile `uuid` can't be mirrored as the default, or None if it can."""
        sized = next((profile for profile, _ in self._copies if profile.uuid == uuid), None)
        if sized is not None:
            return f"it is the sized profile {sized.name!r}; pick another default"
        if not GLib.uuid_string_is_valid(uuid):
            return "not a valid uuid"
        if uuid not in uuids:
            # gnome-terminal has no such profile, so there is nothing to copy. (Preferences
            # may add it to the list a moment later; the next sync looks again.)
            return "not in the profile list"
        return None

    def _set(self, settings: Gio.Settings, key: str, value: GLib.Variant | None) -> bool:
        """Give a key this value (None: reset it to the schema default) if it differs.

        Returns whether it wrote. Writing only real changes matters: gnome-terminal applies
        every write to the tabs using that profile, and every write makes the mirror sync
        again.
        """
        if settings.get_user_value(key) == value:
            return False
        if value is None:
            settings.reset(key)
        elif not settings.set_value(key, value):
            log.warning("can't write %s%s: it is locked", settings.props.path, key)
            return False
        text = value.print_(type_annotate=True) if value is not None else None
        self._writes.add((settings.props.path, key, text))
        return True


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class TerminalWindow:
    """One gnome-terminal window, reached through its GTK actions on D-Bus."""

    bus: Gio.DBusConnection
    bus_name: str  # unique bus name of the gnome-terminal-server that owns the window
    path: str  # e.g. /org/gnome/Terminal/window/1

    def active_profile(self) -> str:
        """The uuid of the active tab's profile."""
        reply = self._call("Describe", GLib.Variant("(s)", (PROFILE_ACTION,)), "((bgav))")
        _enabled, parameter_type, state = reply.unpack()[0]
        if parameter_type != "s" or len(state) != 1 or not isinstance(state[0], str):
            raise UnexpectedReply(f"the {PROFILE_ACTION!r} action takes {parameter_type!r} "
                                  f"and holds {state!r}, not a profile uuid")
        return state[0]

    def set_active_profile(self, uuid: str) -> None:
        """Switch the active tab to the profile with this uuid."""
        self._call("SetState",
                   GLib.Variant("(sva{sv})", (PROFILE_ACTION, GLib.Variant("s", uuid), {})))

    def _call(self, method: str, parameters: GLib.Variant,
              reply_type: str | None = None) -> GLib.Variant:
        # The destination is a unique bus name, which D-Bus never starts a program for,
        # so a call can't launch gnome-terminal.
        return self.bus.call_sync(
            self.bus_name, self.path, ACTIONS_INTERFACE, method, parameters,
            reply_type=GLib.VariantType(reply_type) if reply_type else None,
            flags=Gio.DBusCallFlags.NONE, timeout_msec=DBUS_TIMEOUT_MS, cancellable=None)


class X11Windows:
    """Looks up what a window id stands for: its D-Bus address and its monitor."""

    def __init__(self, display: GdkX11.X11Display) -> None:
        self._display = display
        # GDK has no call that PyGObject can use to read another program's window
        # properties, so this opens its own connection to the same X server.
        self._x = XDisplay(display.get_name())
        self._bus_name_atom = self._x.intern_atom(GTK_BUS_NAME_PROPERTY)
        self._path_atom = self._x.intern_atom(GTK_WINDOW_PATH_PROPERTY)
        self._frame_extents_atom = self._x.intern_atom(GTK_FRAME_EXTENTS_PROPERTY)

    def terminal(self, bus: Gio.DBusConnection, xid: int) -> TerminalWindow | None:
        """The window with this id as a GTK window on D-Bus, or None if it isn't one.

        Raises XError if the window has closed.
        """
        self._drop_events()
        bus_name = self._string_property(xid, self._bus_name_atom)
        path = self._string_property(xid, self._path_atom)
        if not bus_name or not path:
            return None
        return TerminalWindow(bus, bus_name, path)

    def monitor(self, xid: int) -> Monitor:
        """The monitor under the window's visible top-left corner, or the nearest one.

        Raises WindowGone, or XError, if the window has closed.
        """
        try:
            window = GdkX11.X11Window.foreign_new_for_display(self._display, xid)
        except TypeError:  # GDK returns NULL for a window that no longer exists
            raise WindowGone("GDK can't find it") from None
        # GDK treats an X error on its own connection as fatal and exits, and the window
        # can close at any moment, so trap the error instead.
        self._display.error_trap_push()
        try:
            _, x, y = window.get_origin()
        finally:
            failed = self._display.error_trap_pop()
        if failed:  # x and y mean nothing
            raise WindowGone("X error reading its position")
        # Skip the invisible shadow margin of a window with client-side decorations. The
        # origin and the monitor layout are in GDK's (scaled) coordinates, the margin in
        # pixels.
        left, top = self._shadow_margin(xid)
        window_scale = window.get_scale_factor()
        monitor = self._display.get_monitor_at_point(x + left // window_scale,
                                                     y + top // window_scale)
        # Its (whole-number) scale factor turns its geometry back into pixels.
        geometry, scale = monitor.get_geometry(), monitor.get_scale_factor()
        return Monitor(monitor.get_model() or "", geometry.width * scale,
                       geometry.height * scale)

    def _drop_events(self) -> None:
        """Drop the events the X server has sent this connection.

        It never asks for any, but the server sends some to every client anyway (keyboard
        mapping changes), and they would pile up.
        """
        while self._x.pending_events():
            self._x.next_event()

    def _shadow_margin(self, xid: int) -> tuple[int, int]:
        """The left and top shadow margin of a window with client-side decorations."""
        value = self._property(xid, self._frame_extents_atom, X_CARDINAL_FORMAT)
        if value is None or len(value) != 4:  # not the four numbers GTK writes
            return 0, 0
        left, _right, top, _bottom = value
        return left, top

    def _string_property(self, xid: int, atom: int) -> str | None:
        """A string property of a window, or None if it has none."""
        value = self._property(xid, atom, X_STRING_FORMAT)
        return None if value is None else bytes(value).decode(errors="replace")

    def _property(self, xid: int, atom: int, item_bits: int) -> Sequence[int] | None:
        """A property of a window, or None if it has none with items of that size."""
        window = self._x.create_resource_object("window", xid)
        prop = window.get_full_property(atom, X.AnyPropertyType)
        return prop.value if prop is not None and prop.format == item_bits else None


@dataclass
class WatchedWindow:
    """What ProfileSwitcher keeps about a terminal window."""

    # Checks the window SETTLE_MS after its last move; pending while it is moving.
    settle: Debouncer
    # Where its contents were last seen: top-left corner, root coordinates.
    position: tuple[int, int]
    # When it appeared (time.monotonic()), until its placement or first check. Its first
    # position change within PLACEMENT_MS, and before that check, is the window manager
    # placing it, not a move; after the check it counts as a move, so the new position
    # gets checked. (A window opened at a given position is never placed.)
    appeared_at: float | None
    # Whether gnome-terminal ignored a switch and the window gets a second try.
    retried: bool = False


class ProfileSwitcher:
    """Puts every terminal window on the profile its monitor calls for.

    Events only schedule checks. A window that moves is checked SETTLE_MS after its last
    move, by its own timer. Everything else schedules a check of all terminal windows
    (_check_all): SOON_MS ahead for a new window, a change of the "profile" action (a new
    tab, a tab switch, a pick from the menu, this script's own switch) or a profile change
    from the mirror; SETTLE_MS ahead for a monitor change or a second try at an ignored
    switch; ERROR_RETRY_MS ahead after an error. That check skips moving windows, which
    their own timers cover. There are only a few windows, and one pass over all of them is
    simpler than working out which window an event was for.
    """

    def __init__(self, mirror: ProfileMirror, x11: X11Windows, bus: Gio.DBusConnection,
                 wnck_screen: Wnck.Screen, gdk_screen: Gdk.Screen) -> None:
        self._mirror = mirror
        self._x11 = x11
        self._bus = bus
        self._wnck_screen = wnck_screen
        self._gdk_screen = gdk_screen
        # Checks of all windows, one timer per delay, so that a trigger with a long delay
        # can't push back a pending short one. Moving windows have their own timers
        # (WatchedWindow.settle), so a stream of move events can't postpone these either.
        self._soon = Debouncer(self._check_all)
        self._settled = Debouncer(self._check_all)
        self._error_retry = Debouncer(self._check_all)
        # The terminal windows, by window id.
        self._watched: dict[int, WatchedWindow] = {}
        # Errors already logged as warnings; repeats go to the debug log.
        self._warned: set[str] = set()

    def start(self) -> None:
        """Watch the terminal windows open now and from now on, and check them soon."""
        # Wnck reports windows as they open, move and close.
        self._wnck_screen.connect("window-opened", lambda _screen, window: self._watch(window))
        self._wnck_screen.connect("window-closed", lambda _screen, window: self._forget(window))
        for window in self._wnck_screen.get_windows():
            self._watch(window)
        # A monitor plugged in, unplugged, moved or set to another resolution.
        self._gdk_screen.connect("monitors-changed", lambda _screen: self._schedule_settled())
        # gnome-terminal announces changes to the "profile" action's state, which follows
        # the active tab: a new tab, a tab switch, or a profile picked from the menu. From
        # any sender, path and arg0 (the Nones; PyGObject takes no keyword arguments here),
        # because gnome-terminal's unique bus name changes when it restarts.
        self._bus.signal_subscribe(None, ACTIONS_INTERFACE, "Changed", None, None,
                                   Gio.DBusSignalFlags.NONE, self._on_actions_changed)
        self.schedule_soon()

    def schedule_soon(self) -> None:
        """Check all windows that aren't moving, SOON_MS from now."""
        self._soon.trigger(SOON_MS)

    def _schedule_settled(self) -> None:
        """Check all windows that aren't moving, SETTLE_MS from now.

        For a monitor change, and for a second try at a switch gnome-terminal ignored.
        """
        self._settled.trigger(SETTLE_MS)

    def _watch(self, window: Wnck.Window) -> None:
        if window.get_class_instance_name() != TERMINAL_WM_INSTANCE:
            return
        xid = window.get_xid()
        self._watched[xid] = WatchedWindow(settle=Debouncer(partial(self._check_window, xid)),
                                           position=self._position(window),
                                           appeared_at=time.monotonic())
        window.connect("geometry-changed", self._on_geometry_changed)
        self.schedule_soon()

    def _forget(self, window: Wnck.Window) -> None:
        if (watched := self._watched.pop(window.get_xid(), None)) is not None:
            watched.settle.cancel()

    @staticmethod
    def _position(window: Wnck.Window) -> tuple[int, int]:
        x, y, _width, _height = window.get_client_window_geometry()
        return x, y

    def _on_geometry_changed(self, window: Wnck.Window) -> None:
        watched, position = self._watched.get(window.get_xid()), self._position(window)
        if watched is None or position == watched.position:
            # Resized in place, e.g. by a font switch: the monitor under the top-left
            # corner is the same, so there is nothing to check.
            return
        watched.position = position
        appeared_at, watched.appeared_at = watched.appeared_at, None
        if appeared_at is not None and time.monotonic() - appeared_at < PLACEMENT_MS / 1000:
            # Placed by the window manager. Its first check, already scheduled by _watch,
            # reads the position as it is then.
            return
        watched.settle.trigger(SETTLE_MS)

    def _on_actions_changed(self, _bus: Gio.DBusConnection, _sender: str | None, _path: str,
                            _interface: str, _signal: str, parameters: GLib.Variant) -> None:
        # Every GTK application's windows send this signal; only a change of the
        # "profile" state matters. Check the signal's type first: any program can send
        # anything.
        if parameters.get_type_string() != ACTIONS_CHANGED_SIGNATURE:
            return
        if parameters.get_child_value(2).lookup_value(PROFILE_ACTION, None) is not None:
            self.schedule_soon()

    def _check_all(self) -> None:
        """Check every terminal window except the moving ones."""
        for xid, watched in self._watched.items():
            if watched.settle.pending:
                log.debug("window %#x: moving, left to the check after the move", xid)
                continue
            self._check_window(xid)

    def _check_window(self, xid: int) -> None:
        """Check one window, logging its errors so the other windows still get checked."""
        watched = self._watched[xid]
        watched.appeared_at = None  # from now on a position change is a move
        try:
            self._check(xid, watched)
        except (GLib.Error, XError, UnexpectedReply, WindowGone) as error:
            text = describe(error)
            if window_gone(error):  # closed, or gnome-terminal quit, since the event
                log.debug("window %#x is gone: %s", xid, text)
            else:  # a timeout, or gnome-terminal changed: worth knowing, once
                self._warn_once(text, "window %#x: %s", xid, text)
                self._error_retry.trigger(ERROR_RETRY_MS)
        except Exception:
            # A bug. Log it and go on with the other windows rather than skip them.
            log.exception("window %#x: unexpected error", xid)

    def _warn_once(self, key: str, message: str, *args: object) -> None:
        """Log a warning the first time for this key, and at debug level after that."""
        if key in self._warned:
            log.debug(message, *args)
            return
        self._warned.add(key)
        log.warning(message + " (repeats are logged with -v only)", *args)

    def _check(self, xid: int, watched: WatchedWindow) -> None:
        """Switch the window's active tab to its monitor's profile, unless picked by hand."""
        default_uuid = self._mirror.default_uuid
        if not default_uuid:  # no usable default profile: the mirror logged why
            return
        terminal = self._x11.terminal(self._bus, xid)
        if terminal is None:
            log.debug("window %#x has no GTK D-Bus address, skipping it", xid)
            return
        current = terminal.active_profile()
        if current not in self._mirror.managed_uuids:
            log.debug("window %#x: profile %s was picked by hand, leaving it", xid, current)
            return
        monitor = self._x11.monitor(xid)
        profile = profile_for(monitor)
        if profile is None:
            wanted, name = default_uuid, "default"
        else:
            wanted, name = profile.uuid, repr(profile.name)
        where = f"window {xid:#x} on {monitor}"
        if current == wanted:
            watched.retried = False
            log.debug("%s: already on the %s profile", where, name)
            return
        terminal.set_active_profile(wanted)
        if terminal.active_profile() == wanted:
            watched.retried = False
            log.info("%s: switched to the %s profile", where, name)
            return
        # gnome-terminal ignores a profile that isn't in its list yet, e.g. one the mirror
        # has just put back after it was deleted, so the window gets a second try. While
        # the mirror is fighting, the profile may never get there, and each of the
        # mirror's syncs would log the lines below again: quiet_level keeps them at debug
        # level then.
        if not watched.retried:
            watched.retried = True
            log.log(self._mirror.quiet_level(logging.INFO),
                    "%s: gnome-terminal didn't take the %s profile, trying again", where, name)
            self._schedule_settled()
        else:
            watched.retried = False
            log.log(self._mirror.quiet_level(logging.WARNING),
                    "%s: gnome-terminal didn't switch to the %s profile", where, name)


def glib_field_text(field: GLib.LogField) -> str:
    """The value of a field of a GLib message as text (PyGObject gives its bare address)."""
    if not field.value:
        return ""
    # A length of -1 means a NUL-terminated string, for GLib and string_at alike.
    return ctypes.string_at(field.value, field.length).decode(errors="replace")


def log_glib_messages(dconf_level: Callable[[int], int]) -> None:
    """Send the messages of the libraries this uses (GLib, GTK, dconf, ...) to the log.

    Otherwise GLib prints them itself, in its own format and regardless of -v. Info and
    debug messages stay with GLib (GLIB_LOG_LEVELS). `dconf_level` turns the level of a
    dconf message into the one to log it at: dconf warns about every write it fails to
    save, and the mirror keeps redoing them (ProfileMirror.quiet_level).
    """

    def write(level: GLib.LogLevelFlags, fields: Sequence[GLib.LogField], _n_fields: int,
              user_data: None) -> GLib.LogWriterOutput:
        log_level = next((log_level for glib_level, log_level in GLIB_LOG_LEVELS
                          if level & glib_level), None)
        if log_level is None:
            return GLib.log_writer_default(level, fields, user_data)
        text = {field.key: glib_field_text(field) for field in fields
                if field.key in ("GLIB_DOMAIN", "MESSAGE")}
        message = text.get("MESSAGE", "")
        if domain := text.get("GLIB_DOMAIN"):
            message = f"{domain}: {message}"
            if domain == DCONF_LOG_DOMAIN:
                log_level = dconf_level(log_level)
        log.log(log_level, "%s", message)
        return GLib.LogWriterOutput.HANDLED

    GLib.log_set_writer_func(write, None)


class LoggingArgumentParser(argparse.ArgumentParser):
    """An argument parser that logs a bad option, with the prefix, instead of printing it."""

    @override
    def error(self, message: str) -> NoReturn:
        log.error("%s (see --help)", message)
        sys.exit(2)


def hold_lock(path: Path) -> TextIO | None:
    """Lock the lock file and write this process's id in it; None if that fails.

    The lock holds while the returned file stays open, and goes when the process ends in
    any way, so a crash never leaves a stale lock behind.
    """
    try:
        # "a+", not "w": opening must not empty the file while another copy holds it.
        lock = path.open("a+")
    except OSError as error:
        log.error("can't open the lock file: %s", error)
        return None
    try:
        fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
    except BlockingIOError:
        lock.close()
        log.error("already running (%s is locked)", path)
        return None
    # For pgrep/pkill -LF, see the top of this file.
    lock.truncate(0)
    lock.write(f"{os.getpid()}\n")
    lock.flush()
    return lock


def main() -> int:
    # Logging first, so that even a mistyped option (in the autostart file, say) reaches
    # the log with the prefix.
    setup_logging()
    parser = LoggingArgumentParser(
        description="Give each gnome-terminal window the font size of the monitor it is "
                    "on. Runs until stopped; see the top of the script for details.")
    parser.add_argument("-v", "--verbose", action="store_true",
                        help="log every check, not just switches")
    if parser.parse_args().verbose:
        logging.getLogger().setLevel(logging.DEBUG)

    # Wnck needs GTK initialised. Importing Gtk through PyGObject already did that; asking
    # again is harmless and says whether it worked.
    initialised, _argv = Gtk.init_check()
    display = Gdk.Display.get_default()
    if not initialised or not isinstance(display, GdkX11.X11Display):
        log.error("needs an X11 session (no display, or this is Wayland)")
        return 1
    if (problem := settings_problem()) is not None:
        log.error("%s", problem)
        return 1
    if (lock := hold_lock(Path(GLib.get_user_runtime_dir()) / f"{APP_NAME}.lock")) is None:
        return 1

    with lock:
        log.info("started (process %d)", os.getpid())
        mirror = ProfileMirror(SIZED_PROFILES)
        log_glib_messages(dconf_level=mirror.quiet_level)
        try:
            bus = Gio.bus_get_sync(Gio.BusType.SESSION)
        except GLib.Error as error:
            log.error("can't connect to the session bus: %s", error.message)
            return 1
        try:
            x11 = X11Windows(display)
        except DisplayError as error:
            log.error("can't open a second connection to the X server: %s", error)
            return 1
        wnck_screen = Wnck.Screen.get_default()
        wnck_screen.force_update()  # load the current window list now, not on the first event
        switcher = ProfileSwitcher(mirror, x11, bus, wnck_screen, display.get_default_screen())
        # The mirror first: the switcher needs to know the default profile.
        mirror.start(on_profiles_changed=switcher.schedule_soon)
        switcher.start()

        loop = GLib.MainLoop()

        def quit_loop(signum: int) -> bool:
            log.info("stopping (%s)", signal.Signals(signum).name)
            loop.quit()
            return GLib.SOURCE_REMOVE

        # Ctrl+C or a closed terminal (SIGINT, SIGHUP), or logout (SIGTERM): stop cleanly
        # and log it, rather than die silently (or, for Ctrl+C, with a traceback).
        for signum in (signal.SIGINT, signal.SIGHUP, signal.SIGTERM):
            GLib.unix_signal_add(GLib.PRIORITY_DEFAULT, signum, quit_loop, signum)
        loop.run()
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
